 33  │ 33  │                                                                                         
 34  │ 34  │ # This prober doesn't actually recognize a language or a charset.                       
 35  │ 35  │ # It is a helper prober for the use of the Hebrew model probers                         
 36  │ 36  │                                                                                         
 37  │ 37  │ ### General ideas of the Hebrew charset recognition ###                                 
 38  │ 38  │ #                                                                                       
 39  │ 39  │ # Four main charsets exist in Hebrew:                                                   
 40  │ 40  │ # "ISO-8859-8" - Visual Hebrew                                                          
 41  │ 41  │ # "windows-1255" - Logical Hebrew                                                       
 42  │ 42  │ # "ISO-8859-8-I" - Logical Hebrew                                                       
 43  │ 43  │ # "x-mac-hebrew" - ?? Logical Hebrew ??                                                 
 44  │ 44  │ #                                                                                       
 45  │ 45  │ # Both "ISO" charsets use a completely identical set of code points, whereas            
 46  │ 46  │ # "windows-1255" and "x-mac-hebrew" are two different proper supersets of               
 47  │ 47  │ # these code points. windows-1255 defines additional characters in the range            
 48  │ 48  │ # 0x80-0x9F as some misc punctuation marks as well as some Hebrew-specific              
 49  │ 49  │ # diacritics and additional 'Yiddish' ligature letters in the range 0xc0-0xd6.          
 50  │ 50  │ # x-mac-hebrew defines similar additional code points but with a different              
 51  │ 51  │ # mapping.                                                                              
 52  │ 52  │ #                                                                                       
 53  │ 53  │ # As far as an average Hebrew text with no diacritics is concerned, all four            
 54  │ 54  │ # charsets are identical with respect to code points. Meaning that for the              
 55  │ 55  │ # main Hebrew alphabet, all four map the same values to all 27 Hebrew letters           
 56  │ 56  │ # (including final letters).                                                            
 57  │ 57  │ #                                                                                       
 58  │ 58  │ # The dominant difference between these charsets is their directionality.               
 59  │ 59  │ # "Visual" directionality means that the text is ordered as if the renderer is          
 60  │ 60  │ # not aware of a BIDI rendering algorithm. The renderer sees the text and               
 61  │ 61  │ # draws it from left to right. The text itself when ordered naturally is read           
 62  │ 62  │ # backwards. A buffer of Visual Hebrew generally looks like so:                         
 63  │ 63  │ # "[last word of first line spelled backwards] [whole line ordered backwards            
 64  │ 64  │ # and spelled backwards] [first word of first line spelled backwards]                   
 65  │ 65  │ # [end of line] [last word of second line] ... etc' "                                   
 66  │ 66  │ # adding punctuation marks, numbers and English text to visual text is                  
 67  │ 67  │ # naturally also "visual" and from left to right.                                       
 68  │ 68  │ #                                                                                       
 69  │ 69  │ # "Logical" directionality means the text is ordered "naturally" according to           
 70  │ 70  │ # the order it is read. It is the responsibility of the renderer to display             
 71  │ 71  │ # the text from right to left. A BIDI algorithm is used to place general                
 72  │ 72  │ # punctuation marks, numbers and English text in the text.                              
 73  │ 73  │ #                                                                                       
 74  │ 74  │ # Texts in x-mac-hebrew are almost impossible to find on the Internet. From             
 75  │ 75  │ # what little evidence I could find, it seems that its general directionality           
 76  │ 76  │ # is Logical.                                                                           
 77  │ 77  │ #                                                                                       
 78  │ 78  │ # To sum up all of the above, the Hebrew probing mechanism knows about two              
 79  │ 79  │ # charsets:                                                                             
 80  │ 80  │ # Visual Hebrew - "ISO-8859-8" - backwards text - Words and sentences are               
 81  │ 81  │ #    backwards while line order is natural. For charset recognition purposes            
 82  │ 82  │ #    the line order is unimportant (In fact, for this implementation, even              
 83  │ 83  │ #    word order is unimportant).                                                        
 84  │ 84  │ # Logical Hebrew - "windows-1255" - normal, naturally ordered text.                     
 85  │ 85  │ #                                                                                       
 86  │ 86  │ # "ISO-8859-8-I" is a subset of windows-1255 and doesn't need to be                     
 87  │ 87  │ #    specifically identified.                                                           
 88  │ 88  │ # "x-mac-hebrew" is also identified as windows-1255. A text in x-mac-hebrew             
 89  │ 89  │ #    that contain special punctuation marks or diacritics is displayed with             
 90  │ 90  │ #    some unconverted characters showing as question marks. This problem might          
 91  │ 91  │ #    be corrected using another model prober for x-mac-hebrew. Due to the fact          
 92  │ 92  │ #    that x-mac-hebrew texts are so rare, writing another model prober isn't            
 93  │ 93  │ #    worth the effort and performance hit.                                              
 94  │ 94  │ #                                                                                       
 95  │ 95  │ #### The Prober ####                                                                    
 96  │ 96  │ #                                                                                       
 97  │ 97  │ # The prober is divided between two SBCharSetProbers and a HebrewProber,                
 98  │ 98  │ # all of which are managed, created, fed data, inquired and deleted by the              
 99  │ 99  │ # SBCSGroupProber. The two SBCharSetProbers identify that the text is in                
 100 │ 100 │ # fact some kind of Hebrew, Logical or Visual. The final decision about which           
 101 │ 101 │ # one is it is made by the HebrewProber by combining final-letter scores                
 102 │ 102 │ # with the scores of the two SBCharSetProbers to produce a final answer.                
 103 │ 103 │ #                                                                                       
 104 │ 104 │ # The SBCSGroupProber is responsible for stripping the original text of HTML            
 105 │ 105 │ # tags, English characters, numbers, low-ASCII punctuation characters, spaces           
 106 │ 106 │ # and new lines. It reduces any sequence of such characters to a single space.          
 107 │ 107 │ # The buffer fed to each prober in the SBCS group prober is pure text in                
 108 │ 108 │ # high-ASCII.                                                                           
 109 │ 109 │ # The two SBCharSetProbers (model probers) share the same language model:               
 110 │ 110 │ # Win1255Model.                                                                         
 111 │ 111 │ # The first SBCharSetProber uses the model normally as any other                        
 112 │ 112 │ # SBCharSetProber does, to recognize windows-1255, upon which this model was            
 113 │ 113 │ # built. The second SBCharSetProber is told to make the pair-of-letter                  
 114 │ 114 │ # lookup in the language model backwards. This in practice exactly simulates            
 115 │ 115 │ # a visual Hebrew model using the windows-1255 logical Hebrew model.                    
 116 │ 116 │ #                                                                                       
 117 │ 117 │ # The HebrewProber is not using any language model. All it does is look for             
 118 │ 118 │ # final-letter evidence suggesting the text is either logical Hebrew or visual          
 119 │ 119 │ # Hebrew. Disjointed from the model probers, the results of the HebrewProber            
 120 │ 120 │ # alone are meaningless. HebrewProber always returns 0.00 as confidence                 
 121 │ 121 │ # since it never identifies a charset by itself. Instead, the pointer to the            
 122 │ 122 │ # HebrewProber is passed to the model probers as a helper "Name Prober".                
 123 │ 123 │ # When the Group prober receives a positive identification from any prober,             
 124 │ 124 │ # it asks for the name of the charset identified. If the prober queried is a            
 125 │ 125 │ # Hebrew model prober, the model prober forwards the call to the                        
 126 │ 126 │ # HebrewProber to make the final decision. In the HebrewProber, the                     
 127 │ 127 │ # decision is made according to the final-letters scores maintained and Both            
 128 │ 128 │ # model probers scores. The answer is returned in the form of the name of the           
 129 │ 129 │ # charset identified, either "windows-1255" or "ISO-8859-8".                            
 130 │ 130 │                                                                                         
 131 │ 131 │                                                                                         
 132 │ 132 │ class HebrewProber(CharSetProber):                                                      
 133 │ 133 │     SPACE = 0x20                                                                        
 134 │ 134 │     # windows-1255 / ISO-8859-8 code points of interest                                 
