 11 │ 11 │ The textures used for 3D objects in Independence War 2: Edge Of Chaos are in a  
 12 │ 12 │ packed custom format called FTEX. This file format uses file extensions FTC     
 13 │ 13 │ and FTU.                                                                        
 14 │ 14 │ * FTC files are compressed textures (using standard texture compression).       
 15 │ 15 │ * FTU files are not compressed.                                                 
 16 │ 16 │ Texture File Format                                                             
 17 │ 17 │ The FTC and FTU texture files both use the same format. This                    
 18 │ 18 │ has the following structure:                                                    
 19 │ 19 │ {header}                                                                        
 20 │ 20 │ {format_directory}                                                              
 21 │ 21 │ {data}                                                                          
 22 │ 22 │ Where:                                                                          
 23 │ 23 │ {header} = {                                                                    
 24 │ 24 │     u32:magic,                                                                  
 25 │ 25 │     u32:version,                                                                
 26 │ 26 │     u32:width,                                                                  
 27 │ 27 │     u32:height,                                                                 
 28 │ 28 │     u32:mipmap_count,                                                           
 29 │ 29 │     u32:format_count                                                            
 30 │ 30 │ }                                                                               
 31 │ 31 │                                                                                 
 32 │ 32 │ * The "magic" number is "FTEX".                                                 
 33 │ 33 │ * "width" and "height" are the dimensions of the texture.                       
 34 │ 34 │ * "mipmap_count" is the number of mipmaps in the texture.                       
 35 │ 35 │ * "format_count" is the number of texture formats (different versions of the    
 36 │ 36 │ same texture) in this file.                                                     
 37 │ 37 │                                                                                 
 38 │ 38 │ {format_directory} = format_count * { u32:format, u32:where }                   
 39 │ 39 │                                                                                 
 40 │ 40 │ The format value is 0 for DXT1 compressed textures and 1 for 24-bit RGB         
 41 │ 41 │ uncompressed textures.                                                          
 42 │ 42 │ The texture data for a format starts at the position "where" in the file.       
 43 │ 43 │                                                                                 
 44 │ 44 │ Each set of texture data in the file has the following structure:               
 45 │ 45 │ {data} = format_count * { u32:mipmap_size, mipmap_size * { u8 } }               
 46 │ 46 │ * "mipmap_size" is the number of bytes in that mip level. For compressed        
 47 │ 47 │ textures this is the size of the texture data compressed with DXT1. For 24 bit  
 48 │ 48 │ uncompressed textures, this is 3 * width * height. Following this are the image 
 49 │ 49 │ bytes for that mipmap level.                                                    
 50 │ 50 │                                                                                 
 51 │ 51 │ Note: All data is stored in little-Endian (Intel) byte order.                   
 52 │ 52 │ """                                                                             
